.Dd November 20, 2026
.Dt KERNEL_LOCKDOWN 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kernel_lockdown
.Nd the kernel lockdown level
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Lockdown keeps root, as any other caller, from changing the running kernel
or reading its memory, as Linux's lockdown does.
It has three levels:
.Bl -tag -width "confidentiality"
.It none
Nothing is refused, the default.
.It integrity
The operations that could change the kernel are refused with
.Er EPERM :
an open of
.Pa /dev/mem
.Pq see Xr mem 4 ,
a write of a model-specific register through
.Pa /dev/cpu/N/msr
.Pq see Xr msr 4 ,
a write of a PCI function's
.Pa config ,
a write of an I/O BAR's
.Pa resourceN
and a mapping of a memory BAR's
.Pa resourceN
or
.Pa resourceN_wc
.Pq see Xr sysfs 5 ,
as Linux's
.Dv LOCKDOWN_PCI_ACCESS
refuses them, which log
.Ql direct PCI access ,
a write of
.Pa /proc/acpi/call
.Pq see Xr proc 5 ,
whose method can program any device, and which Linux has only from the
unsigned acpi_call module lockdown keeps from loading, logged as
.Ql unsigned module loading ,
and in
.Xr debugfs 5
an open of a file other than one of mode 0444 or less, opened to read,
whose operations have no
.Xr ioctl 2
and no
.Xr mmap 2 ,
and a change of a file's mode, owner or group.
.It confidentiality
What integrity refuses.
.El
.Pp
The kernel command line sets the level at boot with
.Cm lockdown=integrity
or
.Cm lockdown=confidentiality .
.Pa /sys/kernel/security/lockdown ,
mode 0644, shows the three levels with the one in force in brackets:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[none] integrity confidentiality
.Ed
.Pp
Writing the name of a level, with or without a newline, raises the level
to it; the file's mode decides who may write, root alone at 0644, and no
capability is asked, as on Linux.
Nothing lowers the level until the next boot.
Each operation refused logs a line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lockdown: refused: debugfs access is restricted; see man kernel_lockdown.7
.Ed
A write fails with
.Er EINVAL
for text that names no level, and with
.Er EPERM
for a level not above the one in force.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mem 4 ,
.Xr msr 4 ,
.Xr debugfs 5 ,
.Xr sysfs 5
.Sh STANDARDS
The levels, the file and its messages are Linux 6.12's.
Linux's confidentiality level also refuses reads of kernel memory through
facilities this kernel does not have, such as
.Pa /proc/kcore ,
kprobes and BPF.
The file is in a
.Pa /sys
directory here, where Linux mounts securityfs at
.Pa /sys/kernel/security .
Secure Boot does not raise the level.
